		 <td>                            
	                                   

</B><P>&#9;My name is Eshkin, son of Drell. I am not an educated man, although my travels of late have brought me both knowledge and clarity. For my skills with the pen I thank my Erudite friends, Lanzera and Chiada—who have taught me the value of words well spoken.</P><br><P>&#9;Let me begin at the beginning.</P><br><P>&#9;I was born in the hills above Surefall Glade, in the summer of 3140. My father was Drell, the blacksmith of Surefall. My mother, a druid, I am told, died bringing me into this world. My father to this day puts flowers on her grave in the Glade.</P><br><P>&#9;My father was a hard man, but a fair one. His rules were few, but strict. As a boy I worked more than played. Most of my young days were spent at the forge. I suppose it was his way of rearing me without a woman at his side. My earliest memory is of him hammering at the forge and seeing the angry light of the sparks leaping from the steel as he bent it to his will. He never spoke of my mother. I believe it must hurt him very much that she is gone. </P><br><P>&#9;It is a pain I know too well.</P><br><P>&#9;The Druids of the Glade would, for a modest fee, teach the children of the village lessons in reading and arithmetic. It was a fee that we never had trouble coming up with—my father was a very successful smith. However working at the forge, I rarely had the time to attend the lessons.</P><br><P>&#9;It was a rare day in my 13<SUP>th</SUP> year that I first laid my hands on a bow. My father, for the first time, left me with charge of the smithy and the house, to go and visit his sister in West Karana. He would be gone for sixteen days. And while I was of ample age to watch the house and minister myself to my reading and writing lessons, I was not able to run the forge. </P><br><P>In short, my father’s holiday to West Karana was a holiday for both of us.</P><br><P>I was ecstatic. I would leave the house for a fortnight and not look back! I could go down to the glade and listen to the Druids teach. I could fish in the lake with my meager cadre of friends.</P><br><P>The walk down to Surefall Glade is not a short one, but it was good to be out of the smithy. I tucked my wax tablet and stylus under my arm (my father never believed in wasting good coin on parchment) and headed for the Glade.</P><br><P>I arrived to a scene most unexpected. The Druids of the glade were not at their guild tree, but they and many of the Jaggedpine rangers were gathered by the lake, listening enrapt to a stranger speaking on by the bridge.</P><br><P>He was a tall man, lanky of build and bare of face. The lines of his eyes were deep and defined and his gaze set for a great distance. He was an adventurer—the first I had seen. He was a far-traveler, and he wore the dust of the road gladly, almost as a second skin. As he spoke, his eyes were bright, and his smile quick. He was speaking of gnolls. His stories were of danger and intrigue. Grappling in the dark with gnoll raiders for the lives of the prisoners they had taken from the villages in Karana.</P><br><P>His name was Tamryn Scholl and he had come to hunt the gnolls of Blackburrow, at the behest of Antonius Bayle himself.</P><br><P>These were all fine tales, to a boy of thirteen, and tales I would glady tell again. But it was not his tales that took me. Leaning against the rail of the bridge, behind him, was his bow. </P><br><P>It was a thing of mesmerizing beauty. Its graceful curves and even taper were perfectly symetrical. It’s tips were capped in silver, and the material was something I had never seen before. It was black, with a depth of finish that, it seemed to me, pulled in the light and consumed it. It’s grip was polished mother-of-pearl and the silk of it’s string shone like gold.</P><br><P>So enrapt was I with the bow, that I did not notice that Tamryn’s stories had ended. In fact, the only sound was a murmur of muffled laughter—at some joke I had missed. My reverie broken, I glanced about to see everyone staring at me. All the boys of the village and the glade—and the Druids and Rangers as well. Tamryn’s face was lit with bemusement, and I know I flushed red to the gills.</P><br><P>"Boy? Are you still here? Or did someone steal your soul?" He said.</P><br><P>"Sir?" I replied, suddenly more embarrassed. I was never social…in fact I was quite shy. "Uhh."</P><br><P>"Has that trinket caught your eye?" asked Tamryn.</P><br><P>"Uhhhh…yes, sir."</P><br><P>At this point, one of the other boys, whom I knew from my studies (perhaps as the only one less present and more tardy than myself) interrupted. "Hehe! Eshkin never looks a girls like that! Boys sometimes, but never girls!" </P><br><P>This boy’s name was Kartum Trueshot and he was the son of a renowned hunter in far-off Kelethin. He was here at the behest of his father to study with the Jaggepine Rangers. He was a half-elf whose mother was the sister of Hager Sureshot—who moved and married in Kelethin. He had generations of consummate hunters in his bloodline and he was an excellent archer—a fact that he allowed no one to forget. As a ranger, or rather an apprentice ranger, Kartum actually lived in the Glade. He would frequently bully the other boys—myself included. But as I lived in the village, and seldom even attended my lessons in the Glade, I thought little of him. That would change one day.</P><br><P>The Gnoll hunter, spoke up, "Now, boy, don’t be that way. I’m sure...Eshkin, is it? I’m sure he likes girls just fine…" There was a pregnant pause where Tamryn glanced at me and then back down the to bow. He saw something in my eyes I suppose, that caught his interest. "Would you like to hold it, boy?" he asked.</P><br><P>For those of you unfamiliar with the customs of the woodsmen, a Ranger’s bow is a sacred item. Often times, it is passed down, from generation to generation. I am told that the oldest and finest of bows retain the spirits of their owners…a little at time, until they attain a life of their own. I could tell that this was one such bow.</P><br><P>So could everyone else. So could Kartum.</P><br><P>"Uhh…" I was not the most eloquent child.</P><br><P>Tamryn laughed and pressed the bow into my hands. It’s surface was warm…unnaturally so. I fancied I felt it throb in my hands. I fancied, in a strange way…that it liked me. </P><br><P>The bow enchanted me. At the age of thirteen I lay alone on my bed, thinking of all the stories I had heard told, and dismissed as boasting—stories of great hunts, and magnificent battles with elusive quarry. Tales of tracking and exploration, told to me by others over the years, suddenly held new meaning for me.</P><br><P>I was going to be a Ranger.</P><br><P>When my father returned from West Karana, he nodded quietly at the news of my decision. Perhaps there was a bit of sadness in his eyes, at my not choosing to master the forge as he had. He was supportive, however, and sponsored my apprenticeship to Hager Sureshot of Surefall Glade.</P><br><P>If, as I suspect, the bow of Tamryn Scholl guided me to the path of the woodsman, it was not wrong in its estimation of my talent. My newfound passion for archery, swordsmanship and the wilderness erupted. I excelled in my training.</P><br><P>As the years passed, I became a masterful archer, and a passable swordsman (although I still prefer the simple utility of an axe over the flash of a sword). My I had found my place. It was magnificent.</P><br><P>At age 20, I was presented with my laurels, as an initaite Ranger of the Glade. In a gruff, rather unceremonious ceremony, I was given my guild tunic…and sumarily tossed off the bridge, into the lake, by my friends. The fishes in that lake are not friendly, but I was able to climb out mostly intact and smiling, despite several bite marks.</P><br><P>Kartum’s laurels were presented at the same ceremony, and it was this ceremony that finally opened my eyes to a rivalry that had been brewing since the day, seven years before, when Tamryn Scholl came to Surefall Glade.</P><br><P>The thing that I noticed was the difference in the way the other rangers and initiates treated Kartum. There was no cheering and he was not tossed to the fishes—just a few, mild supporters clapping, and the ceremony was over.</P><br><P>It was not because of Kartum’s lineage as a half-elf. There were many half-elves in Surefall Glade. We were all woodsmen, or druids, and we respected each other for that. It was just because…of Kartum.</P><br><P>In retrospect, I chide myself for not paying more attention to politics and such around me. They have never interested me. However, had I been paying attention, I would have noticed then that Kartum was held with great disdain by his peers. </P><br><P>He was a braggart and a bully, when he was younger. But as the years passed, that evolved into something worse. He turned to wine and womanizing, sometimes vanishing for days, whoremongering in Qeynos, when the rest of the initiates were supposed to be patrolling for gnolls and hunting the hills for game to feed the village. Rumors were that he had spent such an extended time in Surefall Glade because his father in Kelethin did not want him back.</P><br><P>Things took a turn for the worse in the winter of 3169. </P><br><P>The snows had been heavy and game was scarce. Hailstorms from the previous season had damaged much of the crops and so we had little reserves. All the initaites were called into service to hunt for the Glade and its outlying villages.</P><br><P>Hager Sureshot was not a man to give orders he himself would not follow, and so was also hunting. This particular frozen day the wind pierced through our cloaks like a driven nail, and dry, stinging snow whipped our faces raw. Our group was five strong; myself, Master Sureshot, Kartum, and two of my fellow initiates. We had hunted far, tracking a herd of elk over several ranges of the Qeynos Hills. At length, with the cold aching through our gloves and boots, we descended through hip-deep snow into a sheltered valley. Our focus had been entirely on the quarry, and the cold and exertion had worn us down.</P><br><P>No one suspected exacly how close we were to Blackburrow.</P><br><P>Master sureshot was in the lead, pressing through the snow after the now clear tracks of our game. The arrows came out of nowhere…hissing hatefully through the air like angry insects. We were taken totally by surprise.</P><br><P>The first volley dropped one of my fellows in his tracks, and Master Sureshot took a shaft through the leg. I felt the air sing as the feathers of one grazed by my face. Cries went up and our small party lurched into action. Master Sureshot staggered back to his feet on his wounded leg and hobbled to the shelter of large group of overhanging rocks. Kartum and the other initiate were close on his heels, as I labored to drag the inert body of our fallen comrade through the snow to the shelter of the rocks. It took me only a few seconds watching his blood pour into the snow to realize that he was dead. Persued by another volley of arrows, I stumbled through the snow to the others.</P>         <br>
